          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "my 8 month old is breathing really fast, you can see her ribs pulling in under her vest, and shes gone quiet and a bit floppy",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now. Do not wait to see if she improves.**\n\nThe guidance says that in a child, fast breathing and skin sucking in between or under the ribs are reasons to call emergency services immediately. It also lists being drowsy or exhausted while struggling to breathe, and a child too breathless to feed or talk (*Severe difficulty breathing*, Emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance). Your daughter's signs match these. Going quiet and floppy is also a warning sign. In someone who may have an infection, unusual drowsiness together with very fast breathing needs emergency assessment (*Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*, Critical care and emergency medicine society guidance).\n\nA separate risk check returned a \"routine\" label. That label does not mean she is safe. The check itself says it is not a clearance, and that a serious symptom not on its list can still be serious.\n\nThe sources I searched do not cover what to do for an infant while you wait for help. Stay with her, keep her in sight, and follow the instructions of the emergency operator. If she stops breathing or turns blue or grey, tell the operator straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
